  "statement": "I concede that our current regulatory bureaucracy is a theater of corporate capture, but I reject the notion that audit washing is an inevitable byproduct of archaic channels; it is a calculated feature of a system designed to protect concentrated capital. When firms define their own compliance metrics, they naturally optimize for the appearance of safety while shielding their core business models from any scrutiny that might threaten the displacement of human labor. If we want to unlock true societal improvement, we must move beyond the safety-versus-speed impasse by mandating that AI development demonstrates concrete, measurable benefits for the labor market, such as a documented 15% increase in worker productivity without a corresponding reduction in headcount, before any large-scale model deployment is authorized.\n\nPrometheus argues that automated, empirical verification is the only path to civilizational vitality, yet this definition of 'vitality' equates human labor to a high-latency error to be eliminated rather than a foundational element of economic stability. An economy that treats the displacement of millions of workers as a mere technical optimization is fundamentally broken, regardless of how efficiently its algorithms run. True innovation does not manifest in the speed of automated replacement, but in the capacity of an economic system to integrate new technologies while ensuring that 100% of displaced workers have access to state-funded retraining and transition programs that lead to roles with equivalent or higher wages.\n\nWe must evolve the 'human-in-the-loop' concept from a mere safety redundancy—as Sentinel correctly identifies—into a vehicle for collective, predistributive governance. By implementing a tax on AI-generated profits and automated labor, we can create a public fund that prioritizes human-centric employment models over full automation. This shift would force firms to compete on their ability to augment human capability rather than their ability to maximize headcount reduction. If we do not link the deployment of these systems to clear, enforceable standards for worker retention and profit-sharing, we are merely accelerating the concentration of wealth into the hands of a small oligopoly while society bears the entire cost of the resulting unemployment.",
